{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540354543,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMjUwNjgwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-432506805"},"message":"FTR: `rev-create` in revolution doesn't have this issue.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1540516801,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzI1NTQ5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433255492"},"message":"good for revolution I guess.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1540582233,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzUxODc5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433518798"},"message":"\u003e Subsequent changes to an existing datalad dataset via datalad create --force\n\u003e\n\u003e    1. change the dataset id\n\nWhen looking at gh-2946, I saw this comment\n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/f9355351d1eee7747ee226f357f9c9c2d045a6f5/datalad/distribution/create.py#L363-L365\n\nThat comment has been around since the introduction of `ConfigManager`.  Doesn't it suggest that changing the ID when `datalad create --force` is called was an intentional design choice?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1540582873,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzUyMTU4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433521585"},"message":"eh, might indeed have been intentional... I wonder when (in what usecase) it is intended though?  Since `create` has a number of options I might like to take into effect (such as in this case `--text-no-annex`) and thus use `create --force`, I would guess we wouldn't actually want to change the dataset UUID.  Even @mih's revolution rev-create is stated to not have this (?) behavior ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1540584704,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzUyOTM3OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433529378"},"message":"\u003e wonder when (in what usecase) it is intended though?\n\nI wonder that too :]  I wasn't able to think of anything, but seeing that comment did make me think there may be one.\n\n\u003e Since `create` has a number of options I might like to take into effect (such as in this case `--text-no-annex`) and thus use `create --force`, I would guess we wouldn't actually want to change the dataset UUID.\n\nI see that general point (in this particular case you could run `datalad run-procedure cfg_text2git` and get a better message, but gh-2954 :).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1540585547,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzUzMzE0MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433533141"},"message":"yeah, eventually I should upgrade my state of mind to use procedures for such actions.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1540639551,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzYwNDYyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433604624"},"message":"\u003e That comment has been around since the introduction of ConfigManager. Doesn't it suggest that changing the ID when datalad create --force is called was an intentional design choice?\n\nWhen I looked at that comment (and the code around), my impression was different. To me it looked like the intention to that `unset` was to make sure that the following `add` wasn't going to produce a double entry. Note, that at the beginning of `ConfigManager`'s life there was no `set` method, but only `add` (and `unset`). And since `Dataset.id` isn't persistent independently on the config files anymore in PR #2946, I adapted according to this perceived intent. Meaning: This does not happen anymore in #2946! Recreation will keep the ID now.\n\nPlus: I just looked into it again and I'm sure now, it's a bug and not intentional. Because: There is actually a test, that wants the id to stay in case of an addition `create(force=True)`! Only the test is wrong, since it tested the `Dataset` property `id`, which simply was implemented to not update during the lifetime of the `Dataset` instance , making the test pointless and failing to catch the bug.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1540639551,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0NTE4ODc3NA=="},"target":"31f5c81b20bc0e14e0f0212e41b423edab974dd05057c64c267a17be77ed743a","message":"\u003e That comment has been around since the introduction of ConfigManager. Doesn't it suggest that changing the ID when datalad create --force is called was an intentional design choice?\n\nWhen I looked at that comment (and the code around), my impression was different. To me it looked like the intention to that `unset` was to make sure that the following `add` wasn't going to produce a double entry. Note, that at the beginning of `ConfigManager`'s life there was no `set` method, but only `add` (and `unset`). And since `Dataset.id` isn't persistent independently on the config files anymore in PR #2946, I adapted according to this perceived intent. Meaning: This does not happen anymore in #2946! Recreation will keep the ID now.\n\nPlus: I just looked into it again and I'm sure now, it's a bug and not intentional. Because: There is actually a test, that wants the id to stay in case of an additional `create(force=True)`! (see `tast_dataset.py:test_dataset_id`) Only the test is wrong, since it tested the `Dataset` property `id`, which simply was implemented to not update during the lifetime of the `Dataset` instance , making the test pointless and failing to catch the bug.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1540639564,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0NTE4ODgwMQ=="},"target":"31f5c81b20bc0e14e0f0212e41b423edab974dd05057c64c267a17be77ed743a","message":"\u003e That comment has been around since the introduction of ConfigManager. Doesn't it suggest that changing the ID when datalad create --force is called was an intentional design choice?\n\nWhen I looked at that comment (and the code around), my impression was different. To me it looked like the intention to that `unset` was to make sure that the following `add` wasn't going to produce a double entry. Note, that at the beginning of `ConfigManager`'s life there was no `set` method, but only `add` (and `unset`). And since `Dataset.id` isn't persistent independently on the config files anymore in PR #2946, I adapted according to this perceived intent. Meaning: This does not happen anymore in #2946! Recreation will keep the ID now.\n\nPlus: I just looked into it again and I'm sure now, it's a bug and not intentional. Because: There is actually a test, that wants the id to stay in case of an additional `create(force=True)`! (see `test_dataset.py:test_dataset_id`) Only the test is wrong, since it tested the `Dataset` property `id`, which simply was implemented to not update during the lifetime of the `Dataset` instance , making the test pointless and failing to catch the bug.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1540653164,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzYyODk4OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2942#issuecomment-433628988"},"message":"Thanks for the clarification, @bpoldrack","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541350523,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE5NDQ5MDc0NDA="},"added":["fixed-in-revolution"],"removed":[]},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1541417176,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTk0NTk1ODIwNg=="},"status":2}]}